
  First, what I found in the repo, because it changes the shape of this conversation:

  index.html   — 11 lines: <h1>Tasks</h1> and an empty <main>

  That's the entire app. One commit, "initial: empty tasks page." There is no task model, no storage, no user concept,
  no server, no build tooling, no tests.

  That matters because every word in your request leans on something that doesn't exist yet:

  - [1m"users"[0m — there's no user concept, so no identity to address a notification to and no way to distinguish "me" from
    "someone else."
  - [1m"tasks they care about"[0m — there's no task record, so nothing to attach a subscription or an assignee to.
  - [1m"change"[0m — there's no write path, so no event ever fires.
  - [1m"get notified"[0m — with no server there's no one to push, and with no persistence a notification can't outlive a page
    reload.

  So a notifications system isn't a layer we can add to this app; roughly 80% of the work is inventing the app
  underneath it. I'm not going to guess at that and hand you a design built on four invented assumptions.

  Rather than pin down notification details first, I want to establish what actually exists. Let me start there.
